Compiler vs Interpreter
Compiler: Converts the entire source code into machine code or bytecode before execution.
Interpreter: Executes code line by line.

In Java:

Java source code is compiled by the Java Compiler into bytecode.
The bytecode is then executed by the Java Virtual Machine (JVM), which acts like an interpreter for bytecode.

What is JVM?
JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine.
It is the runtime environment that:

Executes Java bytecode
Provides platform independence
Handles memory management and security

What is JRE?
JRE stands for Java Runtime Environment.
It contains:

JVM
Core libraries
Runtime components
It is used to run Java programs.

What is JDK?
JDK stands for Java Development Kit.
It contains:

JRE
Compiler (javac)
Debugger
Development tools
It is used to develop Java programs.

Platform Independence
Java is called platform-independent because Java code is compiled once into bytecode and can run on any machine that has a JVM.

This is the main reason Java became popular.

Bytecode
Bytecode is an intermediate code generated by the Java compiler.
It is not machine-specific, so it can run on any platform with JVM support.

Java Program Execution Flow
Source code (.java) → compiled by javac → bytecode (.class) → executed by JVM

Java Fundamentals

1. Basic Structure of a Java Program
A simple Java program looks like this:

public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
    }
}

Important parts:
- public class HelloWorld: every Java program starts with a class
- public static void main(String[] args): entry point of the program
- System.out.println(): prints output to the console

2. Comments in Java
Comments are used to explain code.

// Single-line comment
/* Multi-line comment */
/** Documentation comment */

3. Variables and Data Types
Variables are used to store data.

Syntax:
dataType variableName = value;

Primitive data types:
- byte: small integer
- short: integer
- int: integer (most commonly used)
- long: large integer
- float: decimal numbers
- double: decimal numbers (more precise)
- char: single character
- boolean: true or false

Example:
int age = 25;
double price = 99.99;
char grade = 'A';
boolean isJavaFun = true;

Non-primitive types:
- String
- arrays
- classes
- objects

4. Naming Variables
Rules:
- Must start with a letter, underscore, or dollar sign
- Cannot start with a number
- Cannot use reserved keywords
- Should be meaningful

Example:
int studentAge = 20;
String studentName = "Alice";

5. Operators in Java
Arithmetic operators:
- + addition
- - subtraction
- * multiplication
- / division
- % modulus

Example:
int a = 10;
int b = 3;
System.out.println(a + b);
System.out.println(a % b);

Assignment operators:
- =
- +=
- -=
- *=
- /=

Comparison operators:
- == equal to
- != not equal to
- > greater than
- < less than
- >= greater than or equal to
- <= less than or equal to

Logical operators:
- && AND
- || OR
- ! NOT

6. Taking Input and Printing Output
To read input, Java uses the Scanner class.

Example:
import java.util.Scanner;

public class InputExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
        String name = sc.nextLine();
        System.out.println("Hello " + name);
    }
}

7. Conditional Statements
If-else statement:

int age = 18;
if (age >= 18) {
    System.out.println("Adult");
} else {
    System.out.println("Minor");
}

Switch statement:

int day = 2;
switch (day) {
    case 1 -> System.out.println("Monday");
    case 2 -> System.out.println("Tuesday");
    default -> System.out.println("Other day");
}

8. Loops in Java
For loop:
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    System.out.println(i);
}

While loop:
int i = 0;
while (i < 5) {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
}

Do-while loop:
int i = 0;
do {
    System.out.println(i);
    i++;
} while (i < 5);

9. Methods in Java
Methods are reusable blocks of code.

Syntax:
returnType methodName(parameters) {
    // code
}

Example:
public static int add(int a, int b) {
    return a + b;
}

10. Arrays
Arrays store multiple values of the same type.

Example:
int[] numbers = {1, 2, 3, 4};
System.out.println(numbers[0]);

11. Strings
Strings are used to store text.

Example:
String name = "Java";
System.out.println(name.length());
System.out.println(name.toUpperCase());

Strings are immutable, which means they cannot be changed once created.

12. Object-Oriented Programming Basics
Java is an object-oriented language.

Class and Object:
A class is a blueprint, and an object is an instance of a class.

Example:
class Car {
    String color;

    void drive() {
        System.out.println("Car is driving");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Car c1 = new Car();
        c1.color = "Red";
        c1.drive();
    }
}

Important OOP concepts:
- Class and object
- Encapsulation
- Inheritance
- Polymorphism
- Abstraction

13. Exception Handling
Exceptions are errors that happen during program execution.

Example:
try {
    int result = 10 / 0;
} catch (ArithmeticException e) {
    System.out.println("Cannot divide by zero");
}